{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—pink petals drifting like snow over ancient temples. The air smelled of incense and fresh matcha. I spent mornings wandering Nijo Castle’s silent corridors, then afternoons sipping hojicha at a tiny tea house where the owner smiled without words and bowed deeply when I left.\n\nTokyo was a different rhythm: neon pulses, subway symphonies, and the quiet hum of convenience stores open at 3 a.m. I ate takoyaki from a street cart in Dotonbori, the batter crisp, the octopus tender, sauce and mayo pooling like liquid gold. In Shibuya, I stood in the crossing—thousands moving in perfect, silent choreography—and felt both lost and utterly alive.\n\nI hiked to the Fushimi Inari shrine at dawn, climbing past thousands of vermilion torii gates, each donated by a business hoping for fortune. The higher I went, the quieter it became—until only my breath and the rustle of leaves remained. At the summit, a monk offered me a small wooden ema. I wrote “peace” in shaky kanji and hung it beside hundreds of others.\n\nIn Kanazawa, I learned to fold origami cranes with an elderly woman who’d lost her husband years ago. “Each fold,” she said, “is a prayer.” I didn’t cry then, but I did later, alone in my ryokan, wrapped in a yukata, listening to rain tap the tatami.\n\nJapan doesn’t shout. It whispers—in the clink of a teacup, the rustle of a kimono, the pause between bow and smile. I came for the sights. I left with stillness.\n\nI didn’t bring back much—just a silk fan, a bag of sencha, and a quiet heart.\n\nWords: 298", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.45900171995162964}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.13951724767684937}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.06912066787481308}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.17982004582881927}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0030265499372